Idea: an else-if ladder picks the slab. Each higher slab adds the full cost of the slabs below it.

| Units | Calculation | Bill |
|---|---|---|
| 80 | 80 × 4.00 | Tk 320.00 |
| 150 | 100 × 4.00 + 50 × 5.50 = 400 + 275 | Tk 675.00 |
| 250 | 400 + 100 × 5.50 + 50 × 6.00 = 400 + 550 + 300 | Tk 1250.00 |

#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int units;
    double bill;

    printf("Units used: ");
    if (scanf("%d", &units) != 1 || units < 0) {
        printf("Invalid input\n");
        return 1;
    }

    if (units <= 100)
        bill = units * 4.00;
    else if (units <= 200)
        bill = 100 * 4.00 + (units - 100) * 5.50;
    else
        bill = 100 * 4.00 + 100 * 5.50 + (units - 200) * 6.00;

    printf("Bill: Tk %.2f\n", bill);
    return 0;
}

Sample run: Units used: 250 → Bill: Tk 1250.00

Notes
- The slab limits use <=, so exactly 100 units is billed at Tk 4.00 only (Tk 400.00) and 200 units gives Tk 950.00.
- The bill is double because of the 5.50 rate; int would drop the 50 paisa.
- scanf's return value is checked, so letters or a negative number print Invalid input.

The same logic as a Java method: static double bill(int u) { if (u <= 100) return u * 4.00; else if (u <= 200) return 400 + (u - 100) * 5.50; else return 950 + (u - 200) * 6.00; }

