model_answer_en:
Idea: each term comes from the previous one. term(i) = x^i / i! = term(i-1) × x / i, so there is no separate power or factorial and no overflow of a large factorial.

| i | term = previous × x / i (x = 1) | running sum |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | 1 | 1 |
| 1 | 1 × 1 / 1 = 1 | 2 |
| 2 | 1 × 1 / 2 = 0.5 | 2.5 |
| 3 | 0.5 × 1 / 3 = 0.166667 | 2.666667 |
| 4 | 0.166667 / 4 = 0.041667 | 2.708333 |

#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    double x, term = 1.0, sum = 1.0;   /* term 0 is 1 */
    int n;

    printf("Enter x and the number of terms n: ");
    if (scanf("%lf %d", &x, &n) != 2 || n < 1) {
        printf("Invalid input\n");
        return 1;
    }

    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        term = term * x / i;           /* x^i / i! from the previous term */
        sum += term;
    }

    printf("e^%.2f = %.6f\n", x, sum);
    return 0;
}

Sample runs: x = 1, n = 10 gives e^1.00 = 2.718282; x = 2, n = 15 gives e^2.00 = 7.389056, which matches exp(2) to six decimal places.

Notes
- double is used because the terms are fractions; an int term would become 0 after the first division.
- Computing i! separately overflows int at 13! = 6,227,020,800, while the running term stays small.
- n counts the terms including the leading 1, so n = 1 prints 1.000000.

