Idea: skip blanks, read an optional sign, then for each digit do value = value × 10 + (ch − '0'), because the digit characters '0'…'9' have consecutive codes. Stop at the first non-digit and report failure if no digit was read.

#include <stdio.h>

/* Convert a decimal string to int without atoi()/strtol()/sscanf().
   Accepts leading spaces and one optional sign; stops at the first non-digit.
   Sets *ok to 0 if no digit is found. */
int to_int(const char *s, int *ok) {
    int i = 0, sign = 1, value = 0, digits = 0;

    while (s[i] == ' ' || s[i] == '\t')
        i++;
    if (s[i] == '-' || s[i] == '+') {
        if (s[i] == '-')
            sign = -1;
        i++;
    }
    while (s[i] >= '0' && s[i] <= '9') {
        value = value * 10 + (s[i] - '0');   /* '7' - '0' = 7 */
        digits++;
        i++;
    }
    *ok = digits > 0;
    return sign * value;
}

int main(void) {
    const char *tests[] = {"1207", "  -345", "+42abc", "abc"};
    int i, ok, v;

    for (i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        v = to_int(tests[i], &ok);
        if (ok)
            printf("\"%s\" -> %d\n", tests[i], v);
        else
            printf("\"%s\" -> not a number\n", tests[i]);
    }
    printf("Sum check: %d\n", to_int("1207", &ok) + to_int("  -345", &ok));
    return 0;
}

Output:
"1207" -> 1207
"  -345" -> -345
"+42abc" -> 42
"abc" -> not a number
Sum check: 862

Trace for "1207": 0 × 10 + 1 = 1 → 1 × 10 + 2 = 12 → 12 × 10 + 0 = 120 → 120 × 10 + 7 = 1207. Time O(n). A production version would also check for overflow before value × 10 exceeds INT_MAX.

