def print_first_10(filename):
    try:
        with open(filename, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            for i, line in enumerate(f):
                if i == 10:
                    break
                print(line, end="")
    except FileNotFoundError:
        print("File not found:", filename)

print_first_10("report.txt")

How it works: with open(...) closes the file automatically; iterating over the file object reads one line at a time (so a huge file is not loaded into memory); enumerate counts the lines from 0 and the loop stops after 10; end="" avoids a blank line because each line already ends with a newline. A file shorter than 10 lines is simply printed in full.

C equivalent of the same idea: open with fopen, check NULL, then for (i = 0; i < 10 && fgets(line, sizeof line, fp) != NULL; i++) fputs(line, stdout); and fclose.

