model_answer_en:
Structure: a collection of related variables (members) of possibly different types under one name, each with its own storage, used to model a record such as a student or an account.
Union: a collection of members of possibly different types that share one storage area; only one member is in use at a time, and the size equals the largest member (rounded up for alignment).

Declaring a structure:
struct Student {          /* keyword struct + tag */
    int   id;             /* member declarations */
    char  name[30];
    float cgpa;
};                        /* semicolon required */

Defining variables (three ways):
1. After the declaration: struct Student s1, s2, *p;
2. With the declaration: struct Student { … } s1, s2;
3. With typedef: typedef struct Student Student; Student s3;
Initialisation: struct Student s1 = {101, "Rahim", 3.75f};
Access: s1.id = 102;  p = &s1;  p->cgpa = 3.80f;  (p->cgpa is (*p).cgpa)

Declaring a union:
union Value {
    int   i;
    float f;
    char  ch;
};
union Value v1, *q = &v1;
Initialisation: union Value v1 = {25};  (first member only) or {.f = 2.5f} (C99)
Access: v1.f = 4.5f;  q->i = 7;  (each write replaces the previous member's value)

Rules common to both: the declaration itself allocates no memory; members cannot be initialised inside the declaration (in C); member names must be unique within the type but may repeat in other types; a member may be an array, a pointer, another structure or union; a structure or union cannot contain a member of its own type (only a pointer to it).

