union Value v1, *q = &v1;
Initialisation: union Value v1 = {25};  (শুধু প্রথম member) বা {.f = 2.5f} (C99)
Access: v1.f = 4.5f;  q->i = 7;  (প্রতিটি লেখা আগের member-এর মান প্রতিস্থাপন করে)

দুটির সাধারণ নিয়ম: declaration নিজে কোনো memory নেয় না; declaration-এর ভেতরে member initialise করা যায় না (C-তে); একটি type-এর ভেতরে member-এর নাম অনন্য হতে হবে, তবে অন্য type-এ একই নাম চলে; member হতে পারে array, pointer, অন্য structure বা union; structure বা union নিজের type-এর member রাখতে পারে না (শুধু তার pointer)।

model_answer_en:
General form:
union tag {
    type1 member1;
    type2 member2;
    …
} var1, var2;

Rules:
1. Begin with the keyword union, followed by an optional tag (name of the union type).
2. Declare the members inside braces, each with its type and name and a semicolon; members may be of different types, including arrays, pointers and structures.
3. End the declaration with a semicolon after the closing brace.
4. Variables may be defined in the same statement (after the }) or later: union tag u1, *p;
5. The declaration reserves no memory; each variable gets storage equal to the largest member, rounded up for alignment, and all members start at the same address.
6. Members cannot be given initial values inside the declaration; an initialiser in the variable definition sets only the first member: union tag u1 = {10}; (C99: {.member2 = …}).
7. Members are accessed with . on a variable and -> through a pointer; only the member written last holds a valid value.
8. A union cannot contain a member of its own type (a pointer to it is allowed).
Example: union Value { int i; float f; char ch; } v; v.f = 2.5f; sizeof v is 4.

