model_answer_en:
typedef creates an alias (a new name) for an existing type. It creates no new type and allocates no memory; it makes declarations shorter and more readable and helps portability (typedef unsigned char BYTE;).

With structures:
typedef struct Student {
    int id;
    char name[30];
} Student;
Student s1, *p;          /* instead of struct Student s1, *p; */
The tag (struct Student) and the typedef name (Student) live in different namespaces, so the same word can be used for both. A self-referential structure must still use the tag inside: typedef struct Node { int data; struct Node *next; } Node;

| Point | typedef | #define |
|---|---|---|
| Handled by | compiler | preprocessor (text substitution) |
| Works on | types only | any text: constants, expressions, types |
| Scope | follows normal scope rules | from the definition to the end of the file (or #undef) |
| Pointer aliases | typedef char *PTR; PTR a, b; → both pointers | #define PTR2 char * then PTR2 c, d; → only c is a pointer, d is a char |
| Ends with | a semicolon | no semicolon |

On a 64-bit system sizeof b is 8 and sizeof d is 1 in the example above.

